      "text": "### Octave and the Harmonic Series"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#012",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:music-theory:00-overview, git-history:music-theory:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:music-theory:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "music-theory",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "music-theory",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Music Theory - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Every vibrating body produces a **fundamental** and **overtones** at integer multiples:"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#013",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:music-theory:00-overview, git-history:music-theory:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:music-theory:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "music-theory",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "music-theory",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Music Theory - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nSTRING VIBRATION — HARMONIC SERIES\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nHarmonic   Frequency ratio   Interval above fundamental\n────────   ───────────────   ──────────────────────────\n  1st       f₀  × 1          Unison (fundamental)\n  2nd       f₀  × 2          Octave\n  3rd       f₀  × 3          Octave + perfect fifth\n  4th       f₀  × 4          Two octaves\n  5th       f₀  × 5          Two octaves + major third\n  6th       f₀  × 6          Two octaves + perfect fifth\n  7th       f₀  × 7          Two octaves + minor seventh*\n  8th       f₀  × 8          Three octaves\n  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  *7th harmonic is notably flat relative to 12-TET — hence\n   the \"blue note\" in blues/jazz, and why dominant 7ths feel\n   so natural (7th partial suggests ♭7).\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#014",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:music-theory:00-overview, git-history:music-theory:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "music-theory/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:music-theory:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "music-theory",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "music-theory",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Music Theory - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This harmonic series is the **physical origin** of why a perfect fifth (3:2) and major third (5:4) sound consonant — they lock onto low-numbered partials."
    },
    {
